 @endizza  Sure if you are buying a system right now it's not relevant when the cards were released. But if judging the potential of the arc line itself, which is what the op was saying, then it's very relevant that the card is coming out 2 years after the competitor it looks good against, and it's similarly relevant that within 6 months nvidia will be replacing the 3060 in their lineup with a 4060. The only reason nvidia won't do so sooner than that is because of the glut caused by crypto demand swings, not because of technical issues. these considerations are relevant when judging the seeming potential of the arc line.

intel definitely got a lot of the modern parts of graphics business down: good RT performance, good DX 12 performance, good usage of resizable BAR. But they didn't get the legacy parts down: what if resizable BAR isn't available?, poor with DX 9, 10, and 11 games. More important to the discussion of their potential, their areal (die size) and power efficiency are both atrocious considering the process being used. They are selling these cards with razor thin, if any, margins just to make an impact in the market. They won't do that long term.

In terms of value if you are looking to play just the latest games and are looking for a card right now in this price and performance range, then yes, it could be a very good purchase. In terms of potential, the situation is much more murky. Intel need to improve a lot in their second and third generations to remain around long term.

