 @dream_state  Yes, it's completely hardware-agnostic. As long as your GPU supports Vulkan, you're good. And the GTX 1070 supports Vulkan.

NVIDIA has good DirectX support across the board on Windows though, so you should be just fine without DXVK. In fact, DXVK is likely to introduce pipeline compilation stutter, which native DirectX won't have.

But if a game isn't doing too hot for any reason, you're free to try. I just doubt it'd help much. DXVK's main use is on Linux, to play Windows games that expect DirectX even though it's not available on Linux.

 @Wobbothe3rd  but Intel beats the competition on Vulkan (Doom Eternal and RDR2) hence the remark on Vulkan .. It is also more open to other platforms (mobile, switch, Linux and steamdeck). 
To play legacy titles Intel will need to transform calls to Vulkan too (like DXVK do).. so it makes sense for the card to be efficient with Vulkan.
Of course you can get great performance with D3D12 too but it seems some  developers have more problem to get it right (except MS studios) ..  that being said a bad Vulkan game won't perform better than a good D3D12 port ... but Doom Eternal is a great example of what Vulkan can achieve when engine handle it well.

